nodes.content:
A general construction that maps any real-valued message-passing GNN layer into a quaternion-valued layer is specified by (i) grouping every node feature vector $h_v^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^F$ into $F/4$ quaternions $h_v^{(l),Q}=h_r^{(l)}+h_i^{(l)}\mathrm{i}+h_j^{(l)}\mathrm{j}+h_k^{(l)}\mathrm{k}$ (requiring $F$ divisible by $4$), (ii) representing the layer parameters as a quaternion weight matrix $W^{(l),Q}=W_r^{(l)}+W_i^{(l)}\mathrm{i}+W_j^{(l)}\mathrm{j}+W_k^{(l)}\mathrm{k}$ where $W_r^{(l)},W_i^{(l)},W_j^{(l)},W_k^{(l)}$ are real-valued component matrices, and (iii) performing feature transforms using the Hamilton product $W^{(l),Q}\otimes h_v^{(l),Q}$ so that the transformed components are computed by the block matrix formula
      $$
      \begin{bmatrix}
      \mathcal{R}(W^{(l),Q}\otimes h_v^{(l),Q})\\[6pt]
      \mathcal{I}(W^{(l),Q}\otimes h_v^{(l),Q})\\[6pt]
      \mathcal{J}(W^{(l),Q}\otimes h_v^{(l),Q})\\[6pt]
      \mathcal{K}(W^{(l),Q}\otimes h_v^{(l),Q})
      \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
      W_{r}^{(l)} & -W_{i}^{(l)} & -W_{j}^{(l)} & -W_{k}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
      W_{i}^{(l)} & W_{r}^{(l)} & -W_{k}^{(l)} & W_{j}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
      W_{j}^{(l)} & W_{k}^{(l)} & W_{r}^{(l)} & -W_{i}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
      W_{k}^{(l)} & -W_{j}^{(l)} & W_{i}^{(l)} & W_{r}^{(l)}
      \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix}
      h_{r}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
      h_{i}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
      h_{j}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
      h_{k}^{(l)}
      \end{bmatrix},
      $$
      where $\mathcal{R},\mathcal{I},\mathcal{J},\mathcal{K}$ denote the resulting real and three imaginary component vectors. Using this construction, the MESSAGE, AGGREGATE, and UPDATE functions of any message-passing GNN are replaced by their quaternion counterparts so that the layerwise message-passing semantics are preserved in quaternion space.

steps.reasoning:
Define the feature transform in quaternion space by multiplying quaternion weights with quaternion features using the Hamilton product: $W^{(l),Q}\otimes h_v^{(l),Q}$. Implement this Hamilton product by the following real block-matrix multiplication that produces the four output component vectors $\mathcal{R},\mathcal{I},\mathcal{J},\mathcal{K}$ (where $\mathcal{R}$ denotes the real component vector and $\mathcal{I},\mathcal{J},\mathcal{K}$ the three imaginary component vectors):
$ 
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathcal{R}(W^{(l),Q}\otimes h_v^{(l),Q})\\[6pt]
\mathcal{I}(W^{(l),Q}\otimes h_v^{(l),Q})\\[6pt]
\mathcal{J}(W^{(l),Q}\otimes h_v^{(l),Q})\\[6pt]
\mathcal{K}(W^{(l),Q}\otimes h_v^{(l),Q})
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
W_{r}^{(l)} & -W_{i}^{(l)} & -W_{j}^{(l)} & -W_{k}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
W_{i}^{(l)} & W_{r}^{(l)} & -W_{k}^{(l)} & W_{j}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
W_{j}^{(l)} & W_{k}^{(l)} & W_{r}^{(l)} & -W_{i}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
W_{k}^{(l)} & -W_{j}^{(l)} & W_{i}^{(l)} & W_{r}^{(l)}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
h_{r}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
h_{i}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
h_{j}^{(l)}\\[3pt]
h_{k}^{(l)}
\end{bmatrix},
$ 
thereby producing the transformed quaternion output components from the real component matrices. This block formula is the explicit realization of the Hamilton product for matrix-vector transforms in the network.
